<b>S E C T I O N S

The Terminal window is split into Sections.
(A Dialog is shown sometimes over those sections.)

<c>┌────────────────┬─────────────────┬───────┐
<c>│  Workspace:    :                 │Command│
<c>│  Left-section  :  Right-section  │ panel │
<c>│                :                 │       │
<c>├────────────────┴─────────────────┤       │
<c>│  Display                         │       │
<c>├──────────────────────────────────┤       │
<c>│  Progess-bars                    │       │
<c>│  Status-line                     │       │
<c>└──────────────────────────────────┴───────┘

<b>Workspace

The Workspace contains 2 sections:

<i>1) Left-section
<c>┌────────────┐┌────────────┐┌──────────┐
<c>│ 1·Browser1 ││ 2·Browser2 ││ 3·Player │    -> tab-labels

In the Left-section it is possible to select a Workspace. 
There are 3 Workspaces: Browser1, Browser2 and Player.
Use focus-keys 1 to 3 to select a Workspace. Otherwise, click on the tab-labels.
Left-section shows the selected Workspace's view (Browser1's view is called 
View1; Browser2's view is called View2).
When the Player Workspace is selected, Left-section shows the Player's window.
<i>2) Right-section
<c>│┌────────────┐┌────────────┐┌─
<c>││ 4/6·Fields ││ 7/9·Fields ││   -> tab-labels (fields-tabs)
<c>│
<c>│ 4·Genres                       -> window-label

When a Workspace is selected, Right-section will show the Workspace's Tabs. 
Some Tabs are multi-window: they contain more than 1 window. In this case,
window-labels are printed on top of each window. Press the window's focus-key to
select the window; otherwise, click on the window.
In the example above, the tab-labels for the fields-tabs are printed; below, a
window-label is printed.

<b>Other sections

The Display section prints infos about the playing audiofile, the selected 
Workspace and other things.

The Progress-bars section shows timing infos about the playing audiofile and
the list.

The Status-line shows the full text of the selected item. It is also used to
print error messages (they are blinking).

The Command-panel prints the command menu for the selected window. Most of these
commands are buttons.
Use the arrow-buttons at the top to change between pages. Otherwise, use
"Prev command page" and "Next command page" commands.
The row at the bottom prints infos about the last keyboard or mouse event.

<b>Dialogs

The main Dialog is used to visualize these other windows:

1) Help
2) Settings
3) Equalizer
4) Colors
5) Report

There are other dialogs:
·  Find-dialog
·  Readline-dialog

Readline-dialog is used just to input a line. Find-dialog is more complex. 

<i>Find-dialog

The Find-dialog is used to filter and select one or more items from a list.
Filtering is made by a textual pattern typed by the user.
It is possible to select one or more items. To select more items, use mouse3;
otherwise, use the "Activate item" command.

<b>Selection/activation inside the window

Use arrowUp and arrowDown to select the previous or the next line. Otherwise, 
click on a line with mouse1.

Mouse3 is mainly used to activate a line: it plays a file in the Player; it 
activates/deactivates a field-value in a Field; it sets a directory as 
the working directory in Dirs.

PageUp and pageDown scroll to the previous or the next page. (It is not possible
to scroll lines.) 

When the last line is selected, arrowDown will select the first line.
When the first line is selected, arrowUp will select the last line.

The Home key will always select the first line.
The End key will select the playing audiofile in the Player and the next
activated value in the Fields.

Browser and Player's views print their items folded. Folding is done in either
ways: 

<c>  Artist name · Album name
<c>    audiofile's title (or filename if there is no title)         1  1:23
or
<c>  Composer name · Album name
<c>    audiofile's title (or filename if there is no title)         1  1:23

"1" is the track number, "1:23" is the duration. When duration is 1 hour or
more, it is printed in this format: "1h23" (1 hour and 23 minutes).

At the bottom of the View is the View's status line:

<c>  2/10 [1h23]

"2" is the item's position in the list, "10" is the number of items.
"[1h23]" is the duration of the list.